### Why must you never buffer a large file in memory?

A naive Spring MVC controller that takes a `MultipartFile` and calls `getBytes()` loads the entire file into the heap. A hundred concurrent 100 MB uploads is 10 GB of heap, which is an instant OutOfMemoryError.

```java
package com.bank.upload.antipattern;

import org.springframework.web.multipart.MultipartFile;

public class BufferingAntipattern {

    // WRONG: loads entire file into heap
    public void handleUpload(MultipartFile file) throws Exception {
        byte[] data = file.getBytes(); // 100 MB in heap PER REQUEST
        // 100 concurrent uploads = 10 GB heap -> OutOfMemoryError
    }
}
```

### How do presigned URLs keep bytes off your server?

Your API validates the request and returns a short-lived signed URL. The client uploads directly to S3. Your server never touches the file content.

```java
package com.bank.upload.service;

import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.UUID;

public class PresignedUrlService {

    private final S3Presigner presigner;

    public PresignedUrlService(S3Presigner presigner) {
        this.presigner = presigner;
    }

    public UploadInitResponse initUpload(String userId, String filename,
                                          long sizeBytes, String contentType) {
        if (sizeBytes > 500_000_000) { // 500 MB max
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("File too large: " + sizeBytes);
        }

        String key = "uploads/" + userId + "/" + UUID.randomUUID() + "/" + filename;

        // Presign a PUT URL valid for 5 minutes
        String presignedUrl = presigner.presignPutObject(key,
            Duration.ofMinutes(5), contentType);

        return new UploadInitResponse(key, presignedUrl);
    }
}
```

```text
# Client calls: POST /api/v1/uploads {filename: "loan-docs.pdf", size: 150000000}
# Server returns: {key: "uploads/RAJESH/abc123/loan-docs.pdf",
#                  presignedUrl: "https://s3.amazonaws.com/...?X-Amz-Signature=..."}
# Client uploads 150 MB directly to S3 using the presigned URL.
# Server never touches the file content.
```

### How does S3 multipart upload work for very large files?

The client splits the file into parts (10 MB each), gets a presigned URL per part, uploads parts in parallel, and calls complete. A failed part retries without re-uploading the whole file.

```java
package com.bank.upload.service;

import java.util.List;

public class MultipartUploadService {

    public String initiateMultipart(String key) {
        // Returns an uploadId from S3
        return "upload-id-abc123";
    }

    public String presignPart(String key, String uploadId, int partNumber) {
        // Returns a presigned URL for this specific part
        return "https://s3.amazonaws.com/...?partNumber=" + partNumber;
    }

    public void completeMultipart(String key, String uploadId,
                                   List<PartETag> parts) {
        // S3 assembles all parts into the final object
        System.out.println("Multipart upload complete: " + key
            + " with " + parts.size() + " parts");
    }
}
```

### How do you process metadata asynchronously?

When the upload finishes, drop an event on a queue. Background workers do virus scanning, MIME validation, and thumbnailing.

```java
package com.bank.upload.event;

public class UploadCompletedEvent {
    private String userId;
    private String storageKey;
    private String declaredContentType;
    private long sizeBytes;
    private long timestamp;

    // Published to Kafka topic "upload-events"
    // Consumers:
    //   1. VirusScanWorker -> scans with ClamAV
    //   2. MimeValidator -> checks magic bytes
    //   3. ThumbnailGenerator -> creates preview
}
```

### How does reactive WebFlux stream bytes with backpressure?

When you must stream through your service, WebFlux's `Flux<DataBuffer>` pipes the body in chunks without loading it all into memory. Backpressure signals "slow down" if storage cannot keep up.

```java
package com.bank.upload.controller;

import org.springframework.core.io.buffer.DataBuffer;
import org.springframework.http.codec.multipart.FilePart;
import reactor.core.publisher.Flux;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

public class ReactiveUploadController {

    // WebFlux: body arrives as Flux<DataBuffer>, never fully in memory
    public Mono<String> uploadReactive(FilePart filePart) {
        Flux<DataBuffer> content = filePart.content();
        // Pipe directly to S3 reactive client
        // Only one chunk in memory at a time
        // Backpressure: if S3 is slow, upstream pauses automatically
        return content
            .map(buffer -> {
                // Process chunk (e.g., write to S3)
                int size = buffer.readableByteCount();
                return size;
            })
            .reduce(Integer::sum)
            .map(totalBytes -> "Uploaded " + totalBytes + " bytes");
    }
}
```

### How do you validate MIME type from magic bytes?

Never trust the file extension or declared content type. Read the leading bytes to identify the real format.

```java
package com.bank.upload.validation;

import java.io.InputStream;
import java.util.Set;

public class MimeValidator {

    private static final Set<String> ALLOWED_TYPES = Set.of(
        "application/pdf", "image/jpeg", "image/png");

    public boolean validateMagicBytes(InputStream input) throws Exception {
        byte[] header = new byte[8];
        input.read(header);

        // PDF starts with %PDF (0x25 0x50 0x44 0x46)
        if (header[0] == 0x25 && header[1] == 0x50
            && header[2] == 0x44 && header[3] == 0x46) {
            return true; // genuine PDF
        }

        // JPEG starts with FF D8 FF
        if (header[0] == (byte) 0xFF && header[1] == (byte) 0xD8
            && header[2] == (byte) 0xFF) {
            return true; // genuine JPEG
        }

        return false; // unknown or disallowed type
    }
}
```

### How do you apply rate limiting?

A per-user token bucket prevents one client from flooding storage bandwidth or cost.

```java
package com.bank.upload.ratelimit;

public class UploadRateLimiter {

    // Max 5 uploads per user per minute
    public boolean allowUpload(String userId) {
        int recentUploads = getRecentUploadCount(userId);
        if (recentUploads >= 5) {
            System.out.println("Rate limited: " + userId);
            return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    private int getRecentUploadCount(String userId) {
